\title{High+low-stress job recurrence (+ proof)}
\date{2026-10-28T13:40:32Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\def\OPT{#{\mathit{OPT}}}

\p{The key to solving this problem is to come up with a recursive solution, and then use dynamic programming. The key idea (common to many similar problems) is to consider the most we could make if we worked \em{only} through week #{i} and then stopped. That is, define #{\OPT(i)} = maximum revenue for working weeks #{1 \ldots i}. Now, we can come up with a recurrence for #{\OPT}:
\ol{
  \li{Base cases:
  \ul{
    \li{#{\OPT(0) = 0}. We don’t make any money for working #{0} weeks.}
    \li{#{\OPT(1) = L_1}. We can’t take the high-stress job the first week. (Or maybe we can, in which case #{\OPT(1) = \max(L_1, H_1)}—we need to clarify the problem parameters!)}
  }
  }
  \li{As our inductive/recursive case, ##{\OPT(k) = \max \begin{cases} L_{k} + \OPT(k-1) \\ H_{k} + \OPT(k-2)\end{cases}} First, note that if we are going to maximize the profit for the first #{k} weeks, we should always work the final week (there's no reason to take the week off to prepare for anything; we could always at the very least take the low-stress job). Thus, our decision is between working a high-stress or low-stress job in the final week. Choosing a low-stress job at week #{k} means we should add #{L_{k}} to the optimal profit for the previous #{k-1} weeks and choosing a high-stress job at week #{k} means we had to take week #{k-1} off so we should add #{H_{k}} to #{\OPT(k-2)}. The optimal choice is to choose the max of these two options.}
}
}

\p{Notice that our arguments above actually constitute a proof by strong induction of the recurrence's correctness: we first argue why the base case(s) are correct, then argue why the recursive case for #{\OPT(k)} is correct (\em{assuming}, as our induction hypothesis, that #{\OPT} is already correct for any inputs #{< k}).}
